---
import FoundationsLayout from "../../components/FoundationsLayout.astro";
import SnippetBlock from "../../components/SnippetBlock.astro";
import { snippetFromLiteral, sourceSnippet } from "../../lib/snippet.ts";

const portDeclarations = sourceSnippet("foundations-ports-declarations");
const connectFn = sourceSnippet("foundations-ports-connect");
const graphFn = sourceSnippet("foundations-ports-graph-helpers");
const effectiveFn = sourceSnippet("foundations-ports-effective");
const localPlan = sourceSnippet("foundations-ports-local-plan");
const replicatedPlan = sourceSnippet("foundations-ports-replicated-plan");
const planFn = sourceSnippet("foundations-ports-plan");
const portsFn = sourceSnippet("foundations-components-ports");
const deliverFn = sourceSnippet("foundations-components-deliver");

const flowDiagram = `typed component code             erased host boundary

Output(String) -- encode --> Json
      |                         |
      | output_descriptor       | PortRef + connection ID
      v                         v
Descriptor ---------> EffectiveGraph ---------> Delivery
                                                   |
                         target Input(String) <----+
                                decode + execute: component runtime`;
const flowDiagramSnippet = snippetFromLiteral(
  flowDiagram,
  "text",
  "component port flow",
);
---

<FoundationsLayout
  slug="ports"
  description="Watershed's port foundation uses payload types for direct connections, schema IDs for erased graph data, deterministic validation for an acyclic effective graph, and origin-aware dispatch planning."
>
  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>A port is a component's declared connection point</h2>
    <p>
      Components are written apart from each other. A task list does not
      import a notes panel, and neither one knows the other exists. So the
      wiring between them cannot be code an author writes. It has to be data
      a user can edit while the application runs.
    </p>
    <p>
      A port is what makes that possible. It is a named, typed connection
      point that a component publishes: an output it can send from, or an
      input it can receive on. A <a href="/component-model/components">host</a>{" "}
      reads those declarations, offers the user the connections that are
      legal, and stores the ones the user makes. Neither component ever names
      the other.
    </p>
    <p>
      One port appears in three forms. A host meets the last two; only a
      component author meets the first:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>The typed declaration.</strong> An{" "}
        <code>Output(String)</code> or <code>Input(String)</code> that holds
        the payload type and its codec. Component code holds it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The descriptor.</strong> The same port with the payload type
        erased, leaving the port ID, direction, schema ID, and input class. A
        catalog lists these and a stored graph validates against them.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The reference.</strong> Two strings, an instance ID and a port
        ID. A stored connection is a pair of these plus an edge ID.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Everything below wires the board from{" "}
      <a href="/component-model/components">the last sheet</a>, now with two more
      parts on it: a task list, the notes panel, and an activity log side by
      side. Click a task in the list, and two things should happen. The notes
      panel focuses that task. The activity log records that the task was
      touched.
    </p>
    <p>
      A port has two audiences, and this sheet serves them in order. First
      you <em>use</em> ports: a host reads what the components declare, offers
      the user the connections that are legal, stores the ones they make, and
      routes each event. Then you <em>declare</em> them: the typed Gleam an
      author writes inside one component. Stop after part one if you are
      wiring components somebody else wrote.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="g-block">
    <p class="annot">Part one · using ports</p>
    <h2>Local and collaborative describe the target effect</h2>
    <p>
      The board's two inputs do different kinds of work. Focusing the notes
      panel changes only the screen of the person who clicked. Appending to
      the activity log changes shared state that every peer must see. An
      input declares which of the two it is.
    </p>
    <p>
      <code>LocalInput</code> changes presentation or controller state on the
      client where the event began: focus a subject, select a row, apply a
      filter. <code>CollaborativeInput</code> asks the target component on that
      same client to perform one of its own mutations. Watershed then
      replicates the resulting channel operation in the normal way.
    </p>
    <p>
      The activity log's collaborative declaration lists capabilities such as{" "}
      <code>sequence:insert</code>. A host can show that shared-state effect
      before it stores a connection. The current foundation records the list. A
      separate authorization layer must enforce policy.
    </p>
    <div class="g-note">
      <p>
        <strong>The origin client runs a collaborative input.</strong> It must
        submit the target mutation before it disconnects. The server cannot
        finish a job that no client submitted. Server-run retries would need a
        separate input class with execution records, idempotency, and
        authorization.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>Validate the stored graph before dispatch</h2>
    <p>
      A stored connection carries no Gleam types: it is two{" "}
      <code>PortRef</code> values — an instance ID and a port ID each — plus
      an edge ID.
      By the time it is read back, the board may have moved on. The
      connection can name a deleted notes panel, a retired port, or two
      outputs. Its schemas may no longer match. Two people can also add edges
      that look safe alone and form a cycle once their connection sets merge.
    </p>
    <p>
      <code>port_graph.effective</code> derives the graph a client may use. It
      applies five rules in order:
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>Sort the stored edges by edge ID.</li>
      <li>Reject every duplicate ID.</li>
      <li>Validate both endpoints against the current catalog.</li>
      <li>Accept each surviving edge in that stable order.</li>
      <li>Stop accepting an edge when it would close a cycle.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      The order is what makes this safe. Two clients that read the same stored
      set derive the same acyclic result, with no agreement step between them.
    </p>
    <SnippetBlock snippet={effectiveFn} />
    <p>
      Rejected edges stay in storage. The effective graph returns diagnostics
      for duplicates, missing instances or ports, wrong directions, schema
      mismatches, and cycles. A UI can explain the dropped connection without
      destroying data another client may still understand.
    </p>
    <SnippetBlock snippet={graphFn}>
      <figcaption slot="caption" class="annot">
        the board's two edges, stored as instance and port IDs
      </figcaption>
    </SnippetBlock>
  </div>
  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>Dispatch local intent once</h2>
    <p>
      Now click the task. The click emits the task list's output on one
      client, and the replicated channel change then arrives at every other
      client. Both look like the same event. If both dispatch, every observer
      repeats the activity-log append.
    </p>
    <p>
      <code>dispatch.plan</code> makes the origin explicit. A{" "}
      <code>LocalIntent</code> plan follows the effective graph's stable edge
      order and produces one delivery per edge.
    </p>
    <SnippetBlock snippet={localPlan} />
    <p>
      A <code>ReplicatedChange</code> plan returns no deliveries at all. The
      echo stops at the graph.
    </p>
    <SnippetBlock snippet={replicatedPlan} />
    <p>
      Each <code>Delivery</code> records the trace, edge ID, target port,
      target input class, and encoded JSON payload. The trace lets a host join
      later diagnostics to the click that started them.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>Planning checks the catalog again</h2>
    <p>
      A graph can outlive the descriptor snapshot that validated it. Someone
      can remove the notes panel between validation and the next click. So{" "}
      <code>dispatch.plan</code> re-reads the source and each target before it
      creates deliveries.
    </p>
    <p>
      A missing or redirected port becomes <code>SourceUnavailable</code> or{" "}
      <code>TargetUnavailable</code>. A changed schema becomes{" "}
      <code>SchemaChanged</code>. Valid sibling edges still produce
      deliveries, so the activity log still records the click.
    </p>
    <SnippetBlock snippet={planFn} />
  </div>
  <div class="g-block">
    <p class="annot">Part two · declaring ports</p>
    <h2>Typed declarations stay inside component code</h2>
    <p>
      From here on you are writing a component. Part one read ports as stored
      metadata. This is the typed Gleam that metadata comes from.
    </p>
    <p>
      The task list sends a typed payload out through an output. The notes
      panel and the activity log each receive it through an input. Every
      declaration carries a stable port ID, a stable payload schema ID, and
      one side of the JSON codec. Gleam keeps the payload type attached while
      you hold the port.
    </p>
    <SnippetBlock snippet={portDeclarations} />
    <p>
      The task list's <code>selected</code> output and the notes panel's{" "}
      <code>focus_subject</code> input both carry a <code>String</code>. Both
      name the same <code>watershed/task-id@1</code> schema. The compiler
      rejects a direct connection to <code>Input(Int)</code>.{" "}
      <code>port.connect</code> then checks the schema IDs. That catches two
      string-shaped payloads that mean different things, such as a task ID and
      a line of activity text.
    </p>
    <SnippetBlock snippet={connectFn} />
  </div>
  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>Erase payload types before storage</h2>
    <p>
      The board's wiring outlives the session. It has to be stored, and
      stored data has no Gleam types. This is where the typed value above
      becomes the erased metadata part one read. <code>output_descriptor</code> and{" "}
      <code>input_descriptor</code> remove the payload type. They retain the
      port ID, direction, schema ID, and input class. A mixed component
      catalog lists this metadata, and a stored graph validates it. The typed
      input keeps the decoder a host uses when it executes a delivery.
    </p>
    <SnippetBlock snippet={flowDiagramSnippet}>
      <figcaption slot="caption" class="annot">
        authors keep payload types; hosts store metadata
      </figcaption>
    </SnippetBlock>
    <p>
      A component descriptor carries that erased metadata for every port it
      declares. <code>component.ports</code> is what a host reads to list the
      notes panel's inputs or to check a stored edge against it, with no
      payload type in sight.
    </p>
    <SnippetBlock snippet={portsFn} />
    <p>
      The typed values stay inside component code, and they are what actually
      crosses the boundary. <code>component.emit</code> runs the output's
      codec before the payload leaves. <code>component.deliver</code> finds
      the handler that matches the delivered port ID and decodes before
      component code sees anything.
    </p>
    <SnippetBlock snippet={deliverFn} />
    <p>
      Types protect code written together. Descriptors let the host validate
      data it reads back later — which is every check part one made.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="g-block">
    <h3>The browser runtime follows the whole trace</h3>
    <p>
      Watershed's JavaScript component runtime takes the plan from data to
      execution. For each delivery it:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>decodes the target payload;</li>
      <li>calls the typed component handler;</li>
      <li>stores the handler's next running value;</li>
      <li>queues any outputs the handler emitted;</li>
      <li>remembers which edges this trace already crossed.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      A local input changes only the origin's controller state. A
      collaborative input submits once on the origin. Ordinary channel
      replication carries the mutation to peers without sending it through the
      graph again. <a href="/component-model/workspaces">The workspace layer</a>{" "}
      gives the runtime the persisted manifest and graph it keeps reconciled.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="g-out">
    <span class="annot">The route</span>
    <p>
      Gleam checks payload types for direct connections. After type erasure,
      the host checks schema IDs and descriptors. The effective graph turns an
      invalid stored edge set into one deterministic acyclic view. Dispatch
      plans ordered deliveries for local intent and suppresses a replicated
      echo. Next:{" "}
      <a href="/component-model/workspaces">how a workspace keeps instances, order,
      and wiring in one document without throwing unfamiliar data away</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</FoundationsLayout>
